#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1301bcc-be2b-52ba-9cde-64a578bfaad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^{3} - 2x^{2})Δx + 3x(Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164178} {:answer/id #uuid "1cba7303-3ac3-5872-a973-4f12f5db6797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x^{2} - x^{3})Δx + 3x(Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164179} {:answer/id #uuid "4bef35cd-4ded-5b7c-bc27-71bc037e52f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x^{3} - x^{2})Δx + x(Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164180} {:answer/id #uuid "6022b8fa-b215-5c87-8921-5c5f37ca947c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^{2} - x^{3})Δx + 2x(Δx)^{2} + x(Δx)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164181} {:answer/id #uuid "68603171-1ea1-57b3-ad56-f321f917fa8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^{2} - 2x^{3})Δx + 3x(Δx)^{2} + 4x(Δx)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cba7303-3ac3-5872-a973-4f12f5db6797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x^{2} - x^{3})Δx + 3x(Δx)^{2} + (Δx)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164179} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce506fff-6015-59b1-aad6-be317208e369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "884321dd-6d47-5c66-8194-a61c8e2381b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-331510" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+y)\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{3},$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is $y(x)={x}^{3}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}]-[{x}^{3}+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{3}+(3{x}^{2}-({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}))\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})Δx+3x(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9477a5ac-7e05-5fa4-9cf3-352cc17f2e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Local Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82931969-beeb-522e-9e04-be42c527b26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82931969-beeb-522e-9e04-be42c527b26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343615} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd8265b7-7f1b-5640-835c-16e48338c5c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ba70fe6-872d-5430-adff-3dce55ddc96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343613} {:answer/id #uuid "e92ec6fc-5fa1-550a-bd4b-0cce9dfe1a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e92ec6fc-5fa1-550a-bd4b-0cce9dfe1a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343614} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f6b67ee-28b1-5fa3-8f87-24bf5a07d6db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2caa1575-0417-5abb-9f4e-c6471e7597fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2caa1575-0417-5abb-9f4e-c6471e7597fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343616} :answer-field/id #uuid "282c5bbf-e1db-5969-8acd-eb9c41a56a69" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e52dce56-00d6-5df1-9b61-a11ff1e33cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333163" :question/problem "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.283.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.25)-1.283 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.25}-1.283 \\\\  & ≈0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.644.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.5)-1.644 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5}-1.644 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=2.104.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.75)-2.104 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.75}-2.104 \\\\  & ≈0.013.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | $0.013$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77130223-3730-5a35-bd28-d457f079dad3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the LTE for a Numerical Method Given an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "672bae46-4731-52dc-b2f5-4b65df43e130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "672bae46-4731-52dc-b2f5-4b65df43e130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343619} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee50bf1-2695-5c4e-af02-4baa131d1fcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9a4071c-d689-56c8-a3f6-d39803db33e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333236" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{1},$ for the first step starting at $x=0$ with step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\n${τ}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact $y$-value at the start of the step $x=0$ is $y(0)=0.$ Now, let's find the \n$y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0) & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(0+2)\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0+0.3) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.3)^{2}+2(0.3) \\\\  & =0.045+0.6 \\\\  & =0.645.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the first step starting at $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-(y(0)+Δy) \\\\  & =0.645-(0+0.6) \\\\  & =0.045.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7cc1e18-810d-54cd-90d7-354a865105a7" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression for the LTE for a Given Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009cc582-c9dc-5dba-92f7-d9da46f413c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343622} {:answer/id #uuid "a26e555a-7bb0-5dcc-a020-d4575256afd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (Δx)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343623} {:answer/id #uuid "2afcf363-d921-5005-95d7-0605f64c8708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\cdot (Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343624} {:answer/id #uuid "bc48b2ca-5501-500f-b1aa-45c995cd0275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot (Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343625} {:answer/id #uuid "24cd1ded-2b6c-5d70-8a67-1fe4916162d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009cc582-c9dc-5dba-92f7-d9da46f413c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343622} :answer-field/id #uuid "1934d8bd-782e-5f2c-b60c-edd085705929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8128dafc-bb62-5079-b47a-aed1e51ccf57" :question/math-academy-id "q-333497" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=(3{x}^{2}+5x)^{′}=6x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6x+5)\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =3(x+Δx)^{2}+5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2})+5x+5\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x+5\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x]-[3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+((6x+5)-(6x+5))\\cdot Δx+5x-5x+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8905c41-cedb-5c7b-81f7-7639c5451174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Global Truncation Error Given Numerical Solutions to an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aac334f-203b-5b79-a0f1-333b4d6b296b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aac334f-203b-5b79-a0f1-333b4d6b296b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343628} :answer-field/id #uuid "104ae2a3-f65a-5e06-bca6-4077c900e83d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cde7a55d-6032-522e-b88b-d3d1a3d90fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cde7a55d-6032-522e-b88b-d3d1a3d90fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343629} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b2a15a8-5536-5563-a9b1-34d4fccaa549" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e7ff693-3d18-55c5-8c49-e2ec8da242be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7ff693-3d18-55c5-8c49-e2ec8da242be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343627} :answer-field/id #uuid "50730ef5-8fd0-5222-8d1b-4b60a1b9294e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f0aed5a-c308-5168-979c-ef812c012af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334475" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $0.943$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $0.793$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $0.595$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.3$ is $y(0.3)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.6$ is $y(0.6)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.9$ is $y(0.9)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9).$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $0.943$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3)$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $0.793$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6)$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $0.595$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9)$ |  |\n\nNow we compute the global truncation error at each step, rounding to three decimal places:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3)-0.943 \\\\  & ≈0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6)-0.793 \\\\  & ≈0.032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9)-0.595 \\\\  & ≈0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $0.943$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3)$ | $0.012$ |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $0.793$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6)$ | $0.032$ |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $0.595$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9)$ | $0.027$ |"}]}
